================================================================================
WHAT THIS FILE ESTABLISHES
================================================================================

  Whether the AAP-mandated Opengrep taint A/B (AAP 0.5.1, 0.9.1) still fails
  as QA finding F5 reports it, measured again from scratch in this clone
  rather than read from the arm logs already on disk; and, separately, the
  capability question that A/B exists to answer - whether Opengrep's taint
  engine is actually active on Spark's own Scala.

  It carries five arms this generation ran itself, each with its full argv,
  exit code, UTC start and end instants, elapsed seconds, result count, the
  line of every result, how many results carry a dataflow trace, and its
  SARIF's byte size and sha256; the derived control rule in full with its
  digest; the three contrasts computed from those arms; and two clearly
  separated verdicts - one for the mandated pair, one for the capability
  question.

  An A/B proves a capability only through the CONTRAST between two invocations
  that differ in one setting; enabled-in-config is not proof (AAP 0.1.3). So
  each pair is reported as it came out rather than as it was expected to, and
  nothing was changed to obtain an expected number.

  Companion, machine-readable: harness/artifacts/logs/reverification-f5-taint-ab.json
  Companion, option surface  : harness/artifacts/logs/reverification-f5-taint-engine-options.txt


================================================================================
WHY THIS FILE IS UNDER logs/ AND NEVER UNDER raw/
================================================================================

  Two independent reasons, either sufficient (AAP 0.5.1, 0.6.1). First, raw/
  is RUNNER-ONLY: it receives exactly one artifact per tool that writes one
  and nothing else, ever. An A/B SARIF landing there would overwrite the
  harness/artifacts/raw/opengrep.sarif the Stage 3 runner wrote, and the
  dataset would then be built from an A/B over one file instead of a scan over
  the whole scope. Second, this is one of the run's two deliberate second
  appearances: Opengrep appears here as the taint A/B subject and again as one
  of the nine scanned runners; Joern appears as a runner and again as the
  capability probe's subject. Each second appearance writes outside raw/ and
  contributes NO dataset row (AAP 0.3.2).

  Every file this pass wrote is listed under CONTAINMENT AND CONTINUITY below,
  and all eight are under harness/artifacts/logs/.


