  'traced' counts results carrying a SARIF codeFlow. A `mode: taint` rule has
  no top-level pattern and is evaluated only as source-to-sink reachability,
  so a result from it carries a trace; a plain pattern match emits no
  codeFlow.

  The traced route the capability pair's ON arm reports, read out of its own
  SARIF codeFlow rather than described: Source predicates @ line 351; Call
  convertFilters @ line 351; Propagator filters @ line 645; Propagator flatMap
  @ line 861; Sink s"$name like " @ line 828 and 834. Three method boundaries
  are crossed inside the single file - which is exactly what --taint-intrafile
  adds and what the engine's documented intraprocedural fallback cannot see.


